C = [
("h1", "1. The Office"),
("p", "Claim: a prophet in Israel is the LORD’s appointed spokesman, not a self-made one. The LORD makes himself known to him, puts words in his mouth, and holds the people to account for hearing. Moses is the measure, and Samuel shows an established prophet whom all Israel knew."),
("sub", "1.1 The LORD makes himself known"),
("p", "The first statement of the office is the LORD’s own, spoken at the door of the tabernacle to Aaron and Miriam. “Hear now my words: If there be a prophet among you, I the LORD will make myself known unto him in a vision, and will speak unto him in a dream” (Numbers 12:6). The sentence puts the LORD in the active place. The prophet is the one to whom the LORD makes himself known, and the vision and the dream are the LORD’s means. Nothing in the verse lets a man become a prophet by deciding to, or by being clever about the future."),
("p", "The verse also says where the prophet stands: “among you.” The office is set in the midst of Israel. Numbers 12 does not describe a prophet of the nations or a prophet to all mankind. It describes the way the LORD speaks to the one who is to speak to Israel, and it is spoken in the camp of Israel in the wilderness."),
("sub", "1.2 Moses the measure"),
("p", "The next verses set a standard above the ordinary prophet. “My servant Moses is not so, who is faithful in all mine house. With him will I speak mouth to mouth, even apparently, and not in dark speeches; and the similitude of the LORD shall he behold” (Numbers 12:7, 8). Moses is distinguished from the prophet of the dream and the vision by the directness of the speaking: mouth to mouth, plainly, and not in dark speeches. The LORD then asks Aaron and Miriam why they were not afraid to speak against him."),
("p", "The point for this volume is not a ranking of prophets for its own sake. It is that the LORD himself sets a measure. Whatever else a prophet is, he is compared with the man who received the Law, and a word that contradicts what Moses received does not stand. The tests of Deuteronomy 13 and 18 depend on this. Moses is the one by whom Israel knows the LORD’s commandments, and those commandments are the fixed point against which every later oracle is held."),
("sub", "1.3 Raised up, and the words put in his mouth"),
("p", "Moses himself describes the office to Israel in the plainest terms. “The LORD thy God will raise up unto thee a Prophet from the midst of thee, of thy brethren, like unto me; unto him ye shall hearken” (Deuteronomy 18:15). Three phrases carry the office. He is raised up by the LORD. He comes from the midst of Israel, of their brethren. And Israel is commanded to hearken to him."),
("p", "Moses then says why the office exists. It answers what the people asked at Horeb: “Let me not hear again the voice of the LORD my God, neither let me see this great fire any more, that I die not” (Deuteronomy 18:16). The LORD’s reply to the request is that they had “well spoken” (Deuteronomy 18:17). The prophet therefore stands between a holy God and a people who cannot bear his voice, and the LORD approves the arrangement the people asked for. He promises, “I will raise them up a Prophet from among their brethren, like unto thee, and will put my words in his mouth; and he shall speak unto them all that I shall command him” (Deuteronomy 18:18)."),
("p", "Two limits follow in the same breath. The prophet speaks “all that I shall command him,” which is a limit on what he may add. And the hearer is held to account: “whosoever will not hearken unto my words which he shall speak in my name, I will require it of him” (Deuteronomy 18:19). The office carries authority because the words are the LORD’s, and the same fact limits it. A prophet whose words are his own has no claim on the hearer. Chapter 4 takes up the verses that follow, which deal with the prophet who presumes to speak what the LORD did not command."),
("p", "The Acts Commentary Series reads Deuteronomy 18:15–19 as the promise of the prophet whom Peter and Stephen name in Acts 3 and 7 (Acts Commentary Volume 1). That reading is not re-argued here. This volume asks what the passage says about the office as the prophets practised it, and for that purpose the passage is plain: the prophet is raised up by the LORD, comes from among Israel’s brethren, carries the LORD’s words and no others, and is known by the tests the passage goes on to give."),
("sub", "1.4 Samuel, whom all Israel knew"),
("p", "First Samuel gives a case. “And Samuel grew, and the LORD was with him, and did let none of his words fall to the ground. And all Israel from Dan even to Beersheba knew that Samuel was established to be a prophet of the LORD” (1 Samuel 3:19, 20). The two sentences tie two things together. The LORD let none of Samuel’s words fall to the ground, and all Israel knew that he was established. Recognition by the nation followed the word coming to pass, and the text gives no other ground for it."),
("p", "The next verse names the source: “the LORD appeared again in Shiloh: for the LORD revealed himself to Samuel in Shiloh by the word of the LORD” (1 Samuel 3:21). Samuel does not originate anything. The LORD reveals himself by the word of the LORD, and the word is what Samuel carries. The chapter answers Numbers 12:6 in a living man: the LORD made himself known, and the prophet spoke what he was given."),
("sub", "1.5 What Amos 3:7 says, and what it does not"),
("p", "Amos states the pattern as a principle: “Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets” (Amos 3:7). The volume reads the verse as the LORD’s way with his prophets concerning what he is about to do in Israel’s history. When he is about to act in judgment or deliverance, he tells the prophets first. That is why the prophets could warn, and why Amos can ask, in the verses around it, whether a trumpet can be blown in a city and the people not be afraid."),
("p", "The volume does not read the verse as saying that everything God purposes was told to the prophets. The sentence is about what the LORD “will do” in the course he has announced to his servants, and it says that he reveals it to them before it happens. A secret kept hid in God from the ages is a different matter, and Prophecy & Mystery Volume 2 argues that it was not foretold by the prophets. Amos 3:7 does not contradict that. It describes the announced events of Israel’s history and the LORD’s habit of announcing them."),
("ctbl", "The office in five passages.", [
    ("Passage", "What it says of the prophet", "Verse"),
    ("Numbers 12:6–8", "The LORD makes himself known in vision and dream; Moses is the measure, mouth to mouth", "12:6–8"),
    ("Deuteronomy 18:15–19", "Raised up by the LORD, from the midst of Israel; words put in his mouth; the hearer held to account", "18:15, 18, 19"),
    ("1 Samuel 3:19–21", "None of his words fell to the ground; all Israel knew he was established; the LORD revealed himself by the word", "3:19–21"),
    ("Amos 3:7", "The LORD reveals to the prophets what he will do, before he does it", "3:7"),
    ("What is unsaid", "Whether every prophecy was understood by the one who spoke it; what the Mystery was; how a man in the moment tells a true prophet", "Chapters 2, 4 and 7"),
  ], [0.26, 0.56, 0.18]),
("sub", "1.6 The mouth: Exodus 4"),
("p", "The office has a plain picture at its beginning, before any prophet is named. Moses pleads that he is “slow of speech, and of a slow tongue” (Exodus 4:10). The LORD answers with a question that takes the matter out of Moses’ hands: “Who hath made man’s mouth?” (Exodus 4:11). Then comes the promise: “Now therefore go, and I will be with thy mouth, and teach thee what thou shalt say” (Exodus 4:12). The speaker is sent, the words are taught, and the mouth is the LORD’s to give."),
("p", "When Moses asks again to be excused, the LORD provides Aaron, and the arrangement he describes is the pattern of the whole office in small. “And thou shalt speak unto him, and put words in his mouth: and I will be with thy mouth, and with his mouth, and will teach you what ye shall do” (Exodus 4:15). The word passes from the LORD to Moses, from Moses to Aaron, and from Aaron to the people. “And he shall be thy spokesman unto the people: and he shall be, even he shall be to thee instead of a mouth, and thou shalt be to him instead of God” (Exodus 4:16)."),
("p", "Three things follow, and the volume will hold them through every later chapter. First, the one who speaks is not the author. Aaron is “instead of a mouth,” and a mouth has nothing of its own to say. Second, the words are given beforehand, and the teaching is the LORD’s: “teach thee what thou shalt say.” A prophet does not compose a message and then ask the LORD to bless it. Third, the hearers are not asked to judge Aaron’s eloquence. Moses had said he was not eloquent, and the LORD did not answer that he would become so. The authority of the word did not rest on the quality of the mouth."),
("p", "This is the argument against the claim, still made, that a true prophet is known by the force of his speech or the warmth of his manner. The LORD chose a man who said he could not speak well, and he made a second man to be his mouth. The tests the prophets later give do not examine the speaker’s gifts. They ask where the word came from, where it leads, and whether it stands. Exodus 4 shows why. The mouth was never what made the word true."),
("sub", "1.7 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "A prophet is the LORD’s spokesman, made known and sent by him; no man is a prophet by his own decision.",
    "Moses is the measure, and the commandments received through him are the fixed point against which later oracles are held.",
    "The words are the LORD’s and are put in the prophet’s mouth; the hearer is held to account for hearing them.",
    "Samuel shows the office at work: none of his words fell to the ground, and all Israel knew it.",
    "Amos 3:7 speaks of announced events, not of every purpose of God.",
    "The words are put in the prophet’s mouth, as Exodus 4:12, 15 shows: the one who speaks is the mouth and not the author, and the speaker’s gifts are not the test.",
  ]),
]
